1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,419
A sponge.

2
00:00:01,419 --> 00:00:02,564
A jellyfish.

3
00:00:02,564 --> 00:00:03,710
A cockroach.

4
00:00:04,110 --> 00:00:05,620
One has no organs.

5
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:07,021
One has no head.

6
00:00:07,021 --> 00:00:08,976
One has six jointed legs.

7
00:00:08,976 --> 00:00:10,986
Yet all three are animals.

8
00:00:11,436 --> 00:00:16,464
Diya found all three on one page of
her zoology book, and asked: how do

9
00:00:16,464 --> 00:00:19,138
biologists sort animals this different?

10
00:00:19,488 --> 00:00:22,930
Over a million species of
animals have been described.

11
00:00:23,430 --> 00:00:28,014
By the end, you will know the six body
features used to sort every animal.

12
00:00:28,664 --> 00:00:30,935
So how do you sort a million animals?

13
00:00:31,285 --> 00:00:32,341
Not by looks.

14
00:00:32,341 --> 00:00:37,415
A bat and a crow both fly, but the bat
is a mammal and the crow is a bird.

15
00:00:37,865 --> 00:00:42,653
Biologists look at the body plan instead:
how the body is built, from cells

16
00:00:42,653 --> 00:00:44,275
to layers and cavities.

17
00:00:44,725 --> 00:00:48,081
Shared features become the
bases of classification.

18
00:00:48,081 --> 00:00:50,937
A basis is simply a feature we sort by.

19
00:00:51,587 --> 00:00:54,698
Look closely at the three
animals on Diya's page.

20
00:00:55,048 --> 00:00:58,757
The sponge is a hollow body
with tiny pores, the ostia.

21
00:00:58,757 --> 00:01:00,681
Water flows in through them.

22
00:01:01,181 --> 00:01:03,561
Inside is a cavity, the spongocoel.

23
00:01:03,561 --> 00:01:07,239
Water leaves through the big
opening on top, the osculum.

24
00:01:07,739 --> 00:01:11,082
The jellyfish has a soft bell
with tentacles at its edge.

25
00:01:11,082 --> 00:01:14,923
Cut it through the centre in any
direction, and the halves match.

26
00:01:15,423 --> 00:01:19,978
The cockroach has a head, a thorax
and an abdomen, with jointed legs and

27
00:01:19,978 --> 00:01:21,017
long antennae.

28
00:01:21,517 --> 00:01:23,160
Very different bodies.

29
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:26,909
So biologists ask every
animal the same six questions.

30
00:01:27,559 --> 00:01:29,197
Here are the six questions.

31
00:01:29,547 --> 00:01:30,447
One.

32
00:01:30,118 --> 00:01:31,830
Level of organisation.

33
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:37,001
Do the cells work loosely, or form
tissues, organs and organ systems?

34
00:01:37,751 --> 00:01:38,651
Two.

35
00:01:38,202 --> 00:01:39,102
Symmetry.

36
00:01:38,873 --> 00:01:42,642
Does a cut give two matching
halves, and in how many directions?

37
00:01:43,392 --> 00:01:44,292
Three.

38
00:01:44,002 --> 00:01:44,974
Germ layers.

39
00:01:44,974 --> 00:01:49,508
Does the embryo, the animal at its
earliest stage, build two layers or

40
00:01:49,508 --> 00:01:50,408
three?

41
00:01:50,869 --> 00:01:51,769
Four.

42
00:01:51,411 --> 00:01:52,311
Coelom.

43
00:01:52,050 --> 00:01:56,842
Is there a body cavity between the gut
and the body wall, lined by mesoderm,

44
00:01:56,842 --> 00:01:58,382
the middle germ layer?

45
00:01:59,132 --> 00:02:00,032
Five.

46
00:01:59,707 --> 00:02:00,694
Segmentation.

47
00:02:00,694 --> 00:02:04,755
Is the body built from repeated
segments, like an earthworm?

48
00:02:05,505 --> 00:02:06,405
Six.

49
00:02:06,044 --> 00:02:06,944
Notochord.

50
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:10,337
Does the embryo form a
stiff rod along its back?

51
00:02:10,337 --> 00:02:12,927
It splits chordates from non-chordates.

52
00:02:13,677 --> 00:02:15,802
Biologists also compare systems.

53
00:02:15,802 --> 00:02:19,697
Is the gut a bag with one
opening, or a tube with two?

54
00:02:20,347 --> 00:02:21,397
Remember this.

55
00:02:21,397 --> 00:02:25,233
Animals are sorted by their
body plan, not by their looks.

56
00:02:25,833 --> 00:02:28,786
Now run all three animals
through the questions.

57
00:02:29,136 --> 00:02:30,036
One.

58
00:02:29,651 --> 00:02:34,526
The sponge's cells work loosely, with
no true tissues: the cellular level.

59
00:02:34,526 --> 00:02:36,336
The jellyfish has tissues.

60
00:02:36,336 --> 00:02:38,510
The cockroach has organ systems.

61
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:39,860
Two.

62
00:02:39,493 --> 00:02:41,479
The sponge has no symmetry.

63
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:44,373
The jellyfish is radial: many cuts work.

64
00:02:44,373 --> 00:02:48,658
The cockroach is bilateral: only
one cut gives matching halves.

65
00:02:49,108 --> 00:02:50,008
Three.

66
00:02:49,737 --> 00:02:53,157
The jellyfish builds two
germ layers: diploblastic.

67
00:02:53,157 --> 00:02:55,991
The cockroach builds three: triploblastic.

68
00:02:56,441 --> 00:02:57,341
Four.

69
00:02:57,029 --> 00:02:59,027
The cockroach has a coelom.

70
00:02:59,027 --> 00:03:02,433
The sponge and the jellyfish
have no body cavity.

71
00:03:02,883 --> 00:03:03,783
Five.

72
00:03:03,466 --> 00:03:05,385
The cockroach is segmented.

73
00:03:05,385 --> 00:03:07,105
The other two are not.

74
00:03:07,555 --> 00:03:08,455
Six.

75
00:03:08,108 --> 00:03:10,449
None of them forms a notochord.

76
00:03:10,449 --> 00:03:12,444
All three are non-chordates.

77
00:03:13,094 --> 00:03:14,098
But careful.

78
00:03:14,098 --> 00:03:16,334
One feature alone can fool you.

79
00:03:16,684 --> 00:03:18,369
A jellyfish is radial.

80
00:03:18,369 --> 00:03:21,432
It has two germ layers and no body cavity.

81
00:03:21,882 --> 00:03:26,706
A starfish is radial too, but it has
three germ layers and a true coelom,

82
00:03:26,706 --> 00:03:28,616
and its larva is bilateral.

83
00:03:29,216 --> 00:03:30,116
Your turn.

84
00:03:30,108 --> 00:03:33,063
A jellyfish and a
starfish are both radial.

85
00:03:33,063 --> 00:03:35,125
Are they in the same phylum?

86
00:03:35,125 --> 00:03:36,438
Pause and decide.

87
00:03:39,638 --> 00:03:40,538
No!

88
00:03:40,084 --> 00:03:43,857
The starfish is an echinoderm,
the jellyfish a coelenterate.

89
00:03:43,857 --> 00:03:48,046
The symmetry matched, but the germ
layers and the coelom did not.

90
00:03:48,696 --> 00:03:51,981
Put the answers together, and
they make one branching map.

91
00:03:52,331 --> 00:03:56,135
Cells only, with no tissues:
the sponges, phylum Porifera.

92
00:03:56,585 --> 00:04:00,001
Two germ layers: the coelenterates
and the comb jellies.

93
00:04:00,451 --> 00:04:05,112
Three germ layers, and then the cavity
decides: none in flatworms, a false

94
00:04:05,112 --> 00:04:08,142
one in roundworms, a
true coelom in the rest.

95
00:04:08,592 --> 00:04:13,042
Last, the notochord splits the
true-coelom animals into non-chordates and

96
00:04:13,042 --> 00:04:13,942
chordates.

97
00:04:14,531 --> 00:04:17,756
Now, a worked example: an
earthworm after the rain.

98
00:04:18,106 --> 00:04:20,867
Level: its organs form organ systems.

99
00:04:21,317 --> 00:04:25,281
Symmetry: only one cut, down the
middle, gives matching halves.

100
00:04:25,281 --> 00:04:26,181
Bilateral.

101
00:04:26,478 --> 00:04:28,142
Germ layers: three.

102
00:04:28,142 --> 00:04:29,278
Triploblastic.

103
00:04:29,728 --> 00:04:31,798
Coelom: yes, a true coelom.

104
00:04:31,798 --> 00:04:34,174
Segments: yes, a chain of rings.

105
00:04:34,624 --> 00:04:35,683
No notochord.

106
00:04:35,683 --> 00:04:39,412
So the earthworm is a
non-chordate, in phylum Annelida.

107
00:04:40,062 --> 00:04:41,526
Now, the exam trap.

108
00:04:41,876 --> 00:04:45,926
Many students think a sponge is a
plant, because it stays fixed in one

109
00:04:45,926 --> 00:04:46,826
place.

110
00:04:46,876 --> 00:04:51,206
But a sponge has no cell walls and
no chlorophyll, and it takes its food

111
00:04:51,206 --> 00:04:53,358
from the water flowing through it.

112
00:04:53,358 --> 00:04:54,559
It is an animal.

113
00:04:55,209 --> 00:04:56,615
Where is this asked?

114
00:04:56,615 --> 00:05:00,928
As statement or match questions on a
group's level, symmetry, germ layers

115
00:05:00,928 --> 00:05:01,828
or coelom.

116
00:05:02,349 --> 00:05:03,547
Exam level now.

117
00:05:03,547 --> 00:05:06,673
Match each feature with
the right animal group.

118
00:05:06,673 --> 00:05:07,922
Pause and solve.

119
00:05:13,322 --> 00:05:14,698
The answer is B.

120
00:05:15,198 --> 00:05:17,629
Sponges are at the cellular level.

121
00:05:17,629 --> 00:05:19,713
Coelenterates are diploblastic.

122
00:05:20,213 --> 00:05:23,893
Flatworms have no body cavity,
so they are acoelomates.

123
00:05:23,893 --> 00:05:26,886
And the earthworm shows
metameric segmentation.

124
00:05:27,486 --> 00:05:28,946
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:28,946 --> 00:05:30,447
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:33,647 --> 00:05:36,484
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:36,884 --> 00:05:37,784
One.

128
00:05:37,444 --> 00:05:40,827
Name the six features
used to classify animals.

129
00:05:41,727 --> 00:05:42,627
Two.

130
00:05:42,242 --> 00:05:45,726
Why is symmetry alone not
enough to place an animal?

131
00:05:46,626 --> 00:05:47,526
Three.

132
00:05:47,217 --> 00:05:50,171
Is a sponge a plant or an animal, and why?

133
00:05:51,271 --> 00:05:54,299
Next time: a crowd, a team and a machine.

134
00:05:54,299 --> 00:05:56,819
Do cells work alone, or as a team?

135
00:05:56,819 --> 00:05:59,379
We find out in levels of organisation.
